I’ll first inventory the pattern, metadata, reusable workflows, and candidate structure library. I’ll anchor candidate chemistry and run the required CIF health checks before any refinement, then keep source-model alternatives separate through replayable GSAS-II comparisons.

The chemistry points first to SrWO₄, with residual WO₃/SrCO₃ and charge-balanced non‑1:1 strontium tungstates on the watch list. I’m now extracting observed peak families and enumerating all exact-formula records so a poor database entry cannot be mistaken for exclusion of a plausible phase.

Peak-family indexing has now locked the dominant topology: the 101, 112, 004, 200, 211, 204, 220, 116, 312, 224, 316, 332, 424 and 512 lines all follow scheelite SrWO₄ with one common displacement-like angular trend (position RMS ≈0.010°). I’m constructing two independently sourced scheelite analogues and a lower-symmetry competitor, then I’ll quantify which residual families require WO₃, SrCO₃, or another charge-balanced product.

The inventory challenge is separating cleanly: monoclinic WO₃ drops Rwp from 12.25% to about 7.91% with a stable positive scale. A nominally triclinic WO₃ trial gains only ~0.02% after opening six cell axes, starts worse at the neutral checkpoint, and has not achieved replay-stable shifts, so I’m treating that apparent gain as nuisance/cell coupling pending cross-seeding. The malformed atom-type import in one WO₃ record was detected and is not being used as evidence.

The first reversible profile loop found one supported phase-specific term: WO₃ has replay-stable isotropic size broadening (≈0.140 µm), improving Rwp from 6.935% to 6.713%. SrWO₄ size hit the 10 µm neutral boundary and its strain trial went negative; SrCO₃ size collapsed and its strain was insignificant. Those bound/nonphysical trials are rejected and their altered cells/fractions are not carried forward. I’m restarting from the saved values with only the supported WO₃ term, as required.

The physical baseline is now stable: scheelite SrWO₄ dominates, with monoclinic WO₃ and orthorhombic SrCO₃; only WO₃ needs extra size broadening. I’m testing whether the remaining selective intensity errors are genuine low-index texture rather than compensating scale/background effects, then I’ll replay the winning model and freeze the deliverables.

A diagonal SrWO₄ texture term is strongly supported: [111] lowers Rwp from 6.44% to about 5.10%, converges cleanly, and does not destabilize phase fractions. I’m now challenging that gain against wavelength/profile assumptions and the nearest alternative texture axis; one [112] trial became singular and is being discarded from its untouched checkpoint as required.

The texture survives a full replay. The reported Cu Kα₂/Kα₁ ratio also re-refines to 0.505 (essentially the stated 0.500), while a Kα₁-only model is decisively worse, so the doublet treatment is secure. A one-parameter surface-roughness correction gives a smaller additional gain but is coupled to scale; I’m checking its angular trend independently across all three phases before deciding whether it is physical enough to retain.

The scale-coupled roughness term is replay-stable and corrects the same high-angle bias in independent WO₃ and SrCO₃ families, so it remains provisionally physical. Reopening texture from that checkpoint preserves SrWO₄ [111], and WO₃ shows a separate, stronger [011]-type orientation signature (Rwp ≈4.07%); I’m now testing whether that is robust to the WO₃ source/setting and whether SrCO₃ needs any analogous term.

The orthorhombic SrCO₃ contribution is stable, but its texture gain is much smaller than those of SrWO₄ and WO₃; [001] is the only cleanly converged useful axis, shifting its fraction by only ~0.5 wt%. The common monoclinic WO₃ record still leads the same-symmetry alternate before elaboration; the triclinic competitor is slower because all six metric terms reopen, and I’m letting that comparison finish from the exact same nuisance checkpoint.

The source challenge exposed an important setting issue: the conventional P2₁/n WO₃ record becomes much better with [001] texture (Rwp ≈3.59%), but its geometry carries the previously flagged short O–O contact. Its [001] direction maps to approximately [10−1] in the healthy nonstandard P2₁/c setting—an axis not covered by the first positive-index sweep—so I’m reopening the missing signed low-index axes before accepting any source reversal.

The signed-axis replay resolves the apparent source reversal: the healthy P2₁/c WO₃ record with [10−1] texture gives Rwp ≈3.526%, better than both triclinic P−1 (3.564%) and the geometry-flagged P2₁/n record (3.592%). The phase fractions at this checkpoint also close the Sr:W balance to about 1.003:1, independently supporting the three-phase inventory. I’m doing the final term-pruning/removal challenges now.

The final profile loop changed one modeling choice: once positive common U is admitted, WO₃ microstrain (≈4.67×10³ µstrain) fits reproducibly better than its earlier size term (Rwp 3.401% versus 3.493%). The combined trial drives size back to the neutral limit, so I’m replacing—not stacking—the WO₃ broadening term. SrWO₄ trials remain nonphysical, and SrCO₃ broadening gains are negligible.

The final convergence loop now replays at Rwp ≈3.401% with GOF ≈1.19. Removing WO₃ raises Rwp to 10.63%; removing SrCO₃ raises it to 4.93%, so both minor phases are independently required. The Sr₃W₂O₉ watch model again collapses to zero scale, and the alternative rhombohedral SrCO₃ record is non-significant and replay-unstable. I’m extending the checkpoint to the exact measured endpoints, then exporting and health-checking the frozen files.

The frozen model now spans every measured point and all three exported CIFs pass the required geometry/occupancy health screen. Final crystalline fractions are 58.98% SrWO₄, 25.21% WO₃, and 15.80% SrCO₃; I’m running a last machine check that the JSON, CIFs, profile arrays, and reopened GPX all agree exactly.

Completed. The pattern contains three crystalline phases:

| Phase | Space group | Weight fraction |
|---|---|---:|
| SrWO₄ (scheelite) | I4₁/a | 58.980(137)% |
| WO₃ (monoclinic) | P2₁/c | 25.215(112)% |
| SrCO₃ (strontianite) | Pnma | 15.805(141)% |

The full-range refinement gives Rwp = 3.4005%, Rp = 2.6024%, and GOF = 1.1905. Fractions sum to 1.0 and are normalized over modeled crystalline material. All final CIFs passed the required health screen.

